{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00455 - MONDO_grouped_11786_5324_24332",
  "verbalization": "Loratadine, a second-generation antihistamine, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Allergic Rhinitis through a series of indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Loratadine is associated with Vasomotor Rhinitis, a condition characterized by inflammation of the nasal mucosa due to vascular responses, and this is further linked to Mizolastine and Potassium Iodide, both of which are indicated in the development of Allergic Rhinitis. These relationships imply a potential mechanism where Loratadine may modulate inflammatory responses by targeting histamine receptors, thereby influencing the vascular and mucosal components of allergic rhinitis. The shared indication of Vasomotor Rhinitis across multiple pathways suggests a common underlying mechanism, possibly involving the regulation of vascular tone and mucosal inflammation. While the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated, the integrated evidence points to a role for Loratadine in the pathophysiology of Allergic Rhinitis through its effects on vascular and mucosal inflammation.",
